{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם</b> — meaning, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 78:60) ״אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן בָּאָדָם״, that the essential dwelling of HaShem, blessed be He, is within the souls of His holy people." "And His saying <b>וְלֹא תִגְעַל נַפְשִׁי</b> — and He did not suffice with saying ״וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי״, because HaShem was concerned that Israel might say that this thing that HaShem promises is a matter far removed from reason, that a spiritual permanent dwelling, [in] that which has no purpose of spirituality, should be within human beings possessed of form and matter. And even though HaShem promises to do so, this would be on account of the intensity of the love that transforms the form, in the manner of what the Rashbi explained (Zohar III, 131a) on the verse ״בָּאתִי לְגַנִּי וְגוֹ׳ אָכַלְתִּי יַעְרִי וְגוֹ׳ שָׁתִיתִי יֵינִי״ (Song of Songs 5:1) — a parable of a bridegroom, etc.; one should look there at his words. And it is a thing contrary to the nature of equanimity, and such a thing does not persist continuously like the performance of miracles, and there is no assurance for the continuous persistence of the thing, and the spirituality would recoil from the materiality. Therefore He said <b>וְלֹא תִגְעַל נַפְשִׁי אֶתְכֶם</b>, for HaShem will establish the disposition of their soul in an order such that HaShem dwells within them in the manner accepted in the disposition of one who has achieved equanimity." "And He further said <b>וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי בְּתוֹכְכֶם</b>, to say that besides the fact that He will not recoil, there will also be nachat ruach (contentment of spirit) in the indwelling of His Shekhinah within their midst, and this is the aspect of the soul that is within their innermost being. And the meaning of ״וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי״ is a stroll of delight. It also intends, in the manner of what the Sages of blessed memory said, that the souls of the people of Israel are hewn from His light, blessed be He, and the dispatch of their souls in this world does not cause their separation from their source, for they are attached by the cord of silver as far as the place of their hewing, and through there the light of life descends from the supernal source and joins the holy soul with its source. And this causes the soul to recognize its Creator and to yearn for His service and to walk in His ways. And through man's sin the cord of silver is severed and the light that comes from above does not shine within the soul, and this is the sod of what is said (Isaiah 59:2) ״עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם הָיוּ מַבְדִּילִין בֵּינֵכֶם וּבֵין אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״. And this causes man to crave the pleasures of desire and to lose his passion for the pleasant and sweet intelligibilia. And this is HaShem's utterance in His saying <b>וְהִתְהַלַּכְתִּי בְּתוֹכְכֶם</b> — this is the cord of silver that walks from His light, blessed be He, within man's soul, and through this <b>וְהָיִיתִי לָכֶם לֵאלֹהִים</b>, for such a person will choose to accept the yoke of the divinity of HaShem, blessed be He. And you will find that every soul that sins in the world does not produce harm at its outset until it first precedes with the slightest of the slight sins, and when a man sins even by a hairsbreadth, this aspect of his bond on the supreme level is withheld from him. Go out and learn from the incident of Navot (I Kings 22), that because he uttered a falsehood, even though it was for the purpose of HaShem's will, blessed be He, nevertheless HaShem said to him ״צֵא מִמְּחִיצָתִי״ (Shabbat 149a). And when the evil impulse struggles with man over a fine point until it separates between him and HaShem, then it is able to overpower him to sin great sins — may the Merciful One spare us — which it could not have done before, during the time of the man's attachment to the supernal light." "<b>Further</b>, it appears fitting to explain the passage in this manner: that the verse speaks of the preparations of this world and also of the reward of the World to Come. And it began with the blessing of bread and said <b>וְנָתַתִּי גִשְׁמֵיכֶם בְּעִתָּם</b>, which is the preparation of food and livelihood and security in the land, as He said ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם לָבֶטַח עָלֶיהָ״. Up to here He encompassed all the preparation of this world." "And His saying <b>וְנָתַתִּי שָׁלוֹם</b> etc. — from here begins the hidden reward, and it is from the time of departure from this world, which is the day of death. It is the great fever that is in the heart of every living thing, that bitter cup, for even if a man were whole in every perfection of this world, when he remembers the day of death every joy turns bitter — and how much more so since it has no distant boundary, for no man knows his appointed end. Therefore the One who promises good things to His people came to strengthen their hearts also in this matter, and said that on the day of departure the man will not tremble nor be agitated in the manner of the dying, but HaShem will do a thing such that the departure will be like a man who lies down and whose sleep is sweet to him." "<b>And this is</b> the verse's saying <b>ונתתי שלום</b>, in the manner of the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Shabbat 10a), that the name of HaKadosh Baruch Hu is Shalom, and at the time of a tzaddik's passing His light, may He be blessed, draws near to the person — and this is the sod of death by a kiss. In this manner he will lie down, and this is the verse's saying <b>ושכבתם</b>, as the expression of lying down is used regarding the patriarchs. The sod of the matter is that when the soul senses the drawing near of the pleasant light to it, it yearns and is detached from its place to cleave to the Shekhinah, and because of the intensity of the longing the sweetness increases upon sensing separation so that it does not feel it, and no sensation remains except from the sweetness. See what I wrote at the beginning of Acharei Mot. And all this attainment revolves around engagement in Torah and mitzvot, and this is the verse's statement (Ecclesiastes 5:11): ״מתוקה שנת העובד״ — the 'sleep' is the departure of the soul from the body, and for one who serves HaShem and engages in Torah and mitzvot, the departure of the soul is sweet in the manner described. And the verse's saying <b>ואין מחריד</b> refers to the angel of death, who terrifies a person by his very appearance; and since there are tzaddikim who merit to cleave their soul to the Shekhinah but the angel of death precedes them on account of ailments of the soul to purify it through the distress of terror — therefore He promised that altogether they shall not see [one who] terrifies." "<b>Further,</b> it intends the verse's saying <b>ונתתי שלום</b> — that the body, which is the aspect of earth, will endure and the elements will not separate. Learn this from the incident of Rav Achai bar Yoshiya (Shabbat 152b), whom Rav Nachman felt [in the grave] after several years since his death and saw that he was whole, for HaShem ordains the elements to endure together and not separate, and this is attained through the medium of Torah, as the verse says (Proverbs 6:22): ״בשכבך תשמור עליך״. And the verse's saying <b>ושכבתם ואין מחריד</b> follows their statement (Isaiah 57:2): ״ינוחו על משכבותם״ — that no worm shall rule over their body to terrify them in the grave. You will find that the tzaddikim have no worm in their body; learn this from the incident of Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (Bava Metzia 84a), and similar cases are many. And this matter is a great promise that the body shall rest in the grave, for they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Shabbat 13b): the worm in a corpse's flesh is as painful as a needle in living flesh. And you should know that every created being senses according to the measure of its composition which its Maker established." "And the verse's saying <b>והשבתי חיה רעה מן הארץ</b> — this is the spirit of impurity called חיה רעה, which is evil vitality, the aspect of impurity that rests upon the body after death, for which reason HaShem commanded that one who touches a corpse or enters a tent [of the dead] becomes impure for seven days. HaShem promised that through habituation in Torah and mitzvot, the spirit of impurity will be eliminated from the body. And you will find that they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Ketubot 103a): on the day that Rabbi died, impurity ceased." "And the verse's saying <b>וחרב לא תעבר בארצכם</b>. Scripture gave a reason for the elimination of impurity: it is because this impurity is essentially due to the slaughter of a person by the sword of the angel of death — which is blemished, for through this he becomes a נבלה, and a נבלה is impure and is cast to the dog, as it is written (Exodus 22:30): ״לכלב תשליכון״, and the impurity is the aspect of the dog. But those who walk in the Torah of HaShem as required — ״חרב לא תעבר בארצכם״, which is his body built from the earth. From this inference you know that the sword of the angel of death does not touch the body of a tzaddik. Thus far are the promises regarding the passing and the rest of the body." "And the verse's saying <b>ורדפתם</b>. From here begins the effect of the concealed spirituality attained through engagement in Torah. Know that the intensification of engagement in Torah and the performance of mitzvot is to refine the holy sparks and to unite their branches one to another, which the hold of the kelipot prevents — in the sod of the verse (Isaiah 59:2): ״עונותיכם היו מבדילים ביניכם״ etc. See what I explained in parashat Yitro on the verse (Exodus 19:5): ״והייתם לי סגולה״. The kelipot are called by the name 'haters' and 'enemies.' See what I explained in their statement, may their memory be for a blessing, on the verse (Psalms 92:10): ״כי הנה אויביך ה׳״, and may the words of the living Elohim be sweet to you. Here Scripture informs us that through the medium of engagement in Torah and mitzvot they will pursue their enemies and they will fall etc., and the impurity will perish, as the verse says (Zechariah 13:2): ״ואת רוח הטמאה אעביר״ etc. And the verse's saying <b>לחרב</b> follows the verse's saying (Song of Songs 3:8): ״איש חרבו על ירכו״, and they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Tikkunei Zohar 7), that one who guards His covenant not to touch what he was not permitted — this one has power within him to kill and destroy the enemies of HaShem with the breath of his lips, and therefore it does not say ״בחרב״ but ״לחרב״." "And the verse's saying <b>and five of you shall chase</b> — meaning: from what goes forth from you, five shall chase, they being the five fifths of the Torah; they shall chase a hundred gradations of the kelipot. And this is along the lines of the verse (Proverbs 16:26) ״נֶפֶשׁ עָמֵל עָמְלָה לוֹ״, and the Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 99a): אָדָם בְּמָקוֹם זֶה וְתוֹרָתוֹ עֲמֵלָה לוֹ בְּמָקוֹם אַחֵר — which causes destruction to the enemies of HaShem." "And the verse's saying <b>and a hundred of you</b> — meaning, along the lines of what they said (Menahot 43b): ״מָה HaShem your Elohim asks of you״ etc. (Deuteronomy 10:12) — do not read מָה but rather מֵאָה, these being the hundred blessings. And this is the verse's saying <b>and a hundred of you</b> — meaning, that are made from you, namely the hundred blessings each day — <b>a ten thousand shall they chase</b>." "And He returned to say <b>and your enemies shall fall</b> etc. <b>by the sword</b> — this alludes to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): whoever reads Shema etc., it is as though he grasps a double-edged sword etc., as it is written ״רוֹמְמוֹת אֵל בִּגְרוֹנָם וְחֶרֶב פִּיפִיּוֹת בְּיָדָם״ (Psalms 149:6). Now, the first verse alludes to the promise that derives from action, and the second verse to the promise that derives from sacred speech." "And the verse's saying <b>and I will turn toward you</b> — the verse explains the ultimate success that comes from the destruction of the enemies, and says ״וּפָנִיתִי אֲלֵיכֶם״ — meaning: when this falls, all the sparks of holiness fall back to their root, which is the totality of Israel who gather them. And it terms the sparks of holiness as belonging to Him, blessed be He, since they are the portion of HaShem, and through this they shall be fruitful and multiply. And see what I explained on the verse (Genesis 47:27) ״וַיֵּאָחֲזוּ בָהּ וַיִּפְרוּ וַיִּרְבּוּ מְאֹד״, and what the Magid of Mahariq explained on the verse ״וַיָּמָת יוֹסֵף״ etc. ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל פָּרוּ״ etc." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend in the saying <b>and I will turn</b> etc.: for when the enemy — who is the barrier that separates — is nullified, as it says (Isaiah 59:2) ״כִּי אִם עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם הָיוּ מַבְדִּילִים״ etc., with the removal of the barrier, then HaShem will turn and cleave to us." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that when Israel strives in the matter stated, even though there is an impediment in one portion, the Master will turn to us from the portion He turns, and will magnify and multiply our powers against the enemy until the screen is utterly consumed, and then ״וַהֲקִימֹתִי״ etc." "<b>Further</b>, it intends <b>ופניתי אליכם</b> according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory in the Zohar (Zohar vol. 3, p. 103), for the delight of the righteous who have none above them is at midnight of the second night, when the Holy One, blessed be He, sits upon His throne among the righteous in the Garden of Eden, and removes one partition and reveals to them the sweet inner light. And because of the intensity of the longing and the extraordinary degree of the souls' experience of sweetness, they faint and lights fall away from them — and these are souls that descend to the lower world to illuminate; one may examine his words there in Zohar Hadash (parashat Balak). And this is what is alluded to in the saying ״ופניתי אליכם״, which concerns the great and wondrous delight mentioned. And it uses the language of turning, because the blessed Master does not change, as it is written (Malachi 3:6) ״אֲנִי ה׳ לֹא שָׁנִיתִי״; rather, He reveals from His light whatever He desires and conceals according to what they are not worthy to attain. Therefore He said ״ופניתי אליכם״ — to illuminate toward you the hidden inner light; your eyes shall see and your heart shall rejoice." "And His saying <b>והפריתי אתכם</b> alludes to the sparks that fall away from them because of the fainting, as stated. And His saying <b>והרביתי אתכם</b> means that you should not think that because of the falling away of souls from them their light would diminish, heaven forbid; therefore He said ״והרביתי אתכם״ — that your light will multiply, on the contrary, like the light of the king's face. And the secret of the falling away is perhaps that it happens in one of two ways: either because He multiplies their light until it becomes excessive, or because the preceding light is displaced by a new light — ״וְיָשָׁן מִפְּנֵי חָדָשׁ תּוֹצִיאוּ״ — according to the order stated in the words of the kabbalists, that the mochin of greatness displace the mochin of smallness. Thus what falls away from them is on account of the increase, and this is His saying ״והרביתי אתכם״." "<b>Further</b>, He intends in His saying <b>והרביתי אתכם</b> the language of dominion, and this is along the lines of His saying ״תִּהְיוּ לִי מַמְלֶכֶת כֹּהֲנִים וְגוֹי קָדוֹשׁ״ (Exodus 19:6), which we explained there as exalting them above the angels, the ministers of the Most High — that they would be the intimates of the King and the greatest of the supernal beings. And His saying ״והקימתי״ — here He promised the resurrection, for a covenant is sealed for those who dwell in the dust, that HaShem will raise them from their graves. And He said HaShem, for it is specifically those who learn Torah, along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Ketubot 111a) ״כָּל הָעוֹסֵק בְּטַל תּוֹרָה טַל תּוֹרָה מְחַיֵּיהוּ״. Or perhaps these are the very things He alluded to in His saying <b>והקימתי את בריתי</b>, which is the Torah with you — for the טל that HaShem commands upon the Torah, they are life." "<b>Further</b>, he intended along the lines of what our Sages said (Sanhedrin 90a) regarding the verse ״אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי לָתֵת לַאֲבוֹתֵיכֶם״, that the patriarchs themselves will stand to inherit the land. And this is His saying here, that the very covenant that HaShem swore to establish — the earth forever and ever through their hand — whoever fulfills the conditions of the verse, HaShem will revive him together with the patriarchs, to fulfill through his hand the covenant of the earth forever and ever." "<b>Further,</b> he alluded to the concealment of souls in the bond of life with HaShem, and it is known that the aspect to which the covenant is related is also related to peace — and that is His saying <b>וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי</b> ('I will establish My covenant'), which is the foundation of the world of souls, 'with you.' In this too He included the union of the Life of the worlds with Knesset Yisrael, which is the totality of the souls, and the discerning one will understand that this is the ultimate purpose of the rest of the souls." "And His saying <b>וַאֲכַלְתֶּם יָשָׁן נוֹשָׁן</b> ('and you shall eat old store'). Here He alluded to the feast of the World to Come, that HaShem will let them taste of the wine preserved in its grapes from the six days of Genesis, and also of the flesh of the Leviathan. And there is no 'old store' (יָשָׁן נוֹשָׁן) like this, which nothing in the world preceded before the creation of man." "And His saying <b>וְיָשָׁן מִפְּנֵי חָדָשׁ תּוֹצִיאוּ</b> ('and you shall bring out the old because of the new'). Besides what I explained concerning it above, it further alludes, along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Hagigah 12a) on the verse ״וְיִמָּנַע מֵרְשָׁעִים אוֹרָם״ (Job 38:15) — that the light with which the Holy One, blessed be He, created the work of Genesis, the Holy One, blessed be He, stored away for the righteous, because He saw the wicked who are not worthy to use it, and so on. And that is His saying ״וְיָשָׁן״ — meaning the light that preceded this new light that came into being after it. And that is His saying ״מִפְּנֵי חָדָשׁ תּוֹצִיאוּ״ — meaning, you shall bring it out from its storehouses to use it." "And His saying <b>וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי</b> etc. ('I will set My dwelling'). It intends, along the lines of the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Batra 75a), that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to bring down a dwelling from heaven. And there are among our Sages of blessed memory who said that it is of sapphire, and there are among our Sages of blessed memory who said it is of fire — as His saying (Exodus 15:17) ״פָּעַלְתָּ יהוה מִקְּדָשׁ יהוה כּוֹנְנוּ יָדֶיךָ״. And that is His saying <b>וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם</b> — meaning, the dwelling whose foundations My hands established, I will bring it down and set it in your midst. And from then forever the matter shall stand, as His saying ״וְלֹא תִגְעַל נַפְשִׁי אֶתְכֶם״ — for He will remove the spirit of impurity from the earth." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of their statement of blessed memory on the verse (Numbers 23:23) ״כָּעֵת יֵאָמֵר לְיַעֲקֹב״ etc. — that the angels are outside and the righteous are inside, and the angels ask the righteous what El has done. And that is His saying <b>וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם</b> — that the camp of the angels will not interrupt between you and Me." "And His saying <b>and I will walk in your midst.</b> This is along the lines of His saying (Joel 3:1) ״וְנִבְּאוּ בְנֵיכֶם וּבְנוֹתֵיכֶם״ — that His light, blessed be He, will walk among them, and this is the spirit of prophecy, along the lines of what is said (Numbers 11:29) ״עַם ה׳ נְבִיאִים כִּי יִתֵּן ה׳ אֶת רוּחוֹ עֲלֵיהֶם״:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of what the Sages said, may their memory be for blessing (Ta'anit 31a), that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make a dance for the righteous, and HaShem will sit among them, and each one will point with his finger and say ״זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ״. And this is His saying <b>and I will walk in your midst.</b> And it uses the language of ״walking״ because the walking of His light, blessed be He, to nourish the souls of those seated around Him is called ״walking.״ And His saying <b>and I will be for you for Elohim</b> is their statement ״זֶה אֵלִי וְאַנְוֵהוּ״ — or perhaps it alludes to the word נוה (navé, dwelling), that the righteous themselves are the dwelling of the Holy One, blessed be He, for He dwells among them. And His saying <b>and you shall be for Me for a people</b> means that they will be closer than all the host of the heavens." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by His saying <b>and I will be for you for Elohim</b>, that in this present time the faith in His divinity comes from the mouth of the prophets. Therefore He said that behold, days are coming when all of HaShem's people will recognize His divinity, blessed be He, and they will know that YHWH is the Elohim, and this is the meaning of ״and I will be for you for Elohim״ — that they will recognize His divinity from themselves. And since this recognition will exist for all created beings, as it is written (Zechariah 14:9) ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא יִהְיֶה ה׳ אֶחָד״, therefore He said <b>and you shall be for Me for a people</b> — even though the recognition will be equal for all, none will be to Him for a people except the people of Israel." "And His saying <b>I am YHWH, etc.</b> It may be explained along the lines of the statement of the Sages (Berakhot 12a) regarding the verse ״וְלֹא יֵאָמֵר עוֹד חַי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלָה אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרַיִם״ etc. (Jeremiah 23:7), that the intent is that they will say ״חַי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלָה יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם וּמִכָּל הָאֲרָצוֹת״. And this is His saying ״I am YHWH, etc., who brought you out of the land of Egypt״ — this corresponds to the exodus from Egypt; and corresponding to ״who brought you up from all the lands״ He said ״וָאֶשְׁבֹּר מוֹטוֹת עֻלְּכֶם״ etc. And according to what I explained on the verse (Exodus 20:2) ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם מִבֵּית עֲבָדִים״, the verse may also be explained in the same manner; see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {24 "The citation of Numbers 23:23 appears to reference the Talmudic discussion (Bava Batra 75a) where this verse is interpreted in the context of the angels' question; the reference is to the Sages' exposition rather than the plain sense of the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:03:51.008106024Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}